## About the Collaborative Sciences Project

The Collaborative Sciences Project (CSP) is a compulsory interdisciplinary science activity in the IB Diploma Programme. Students work in teams across science disciplines to investigate a local manifestation of a global issue, applying subject knowledge through scientific inquiry, experimentation, and research. The project is process-focused rather than product-focused, and students consider environmental, social, and ethical implications of their findings. Each project culminates in a presentation to the school community, followed by a short reflection.

## Group 1: Comparative Analysis of Menstrual Products

**Members:** Hui Ru Yang, Anna Udicova, Ela Sabolova, Milana Golubkova, Alexander Hvezdon Stefko

**Global issue:** Women's health and environmental sustainability

This group compared menstrual products (tampons, commercial pads, organic pads, and reusable cloth pads) across five scientific disciplines to determine which product type minimizes health risks and environmental harm, and to recommend safer options.

### Disciplines and methods

- **Biology:** Reviewed published literature on bacterial growth, chemical exposure, Toxic Shock Syndrome risk, and skin irritation across product types. Tampons carry the highest bacterial and TSS risk because internal use introduces oxygen into the vagina.
- **Chemistry:** Analyzed the chemical composition of pads and tampons, identifying base fibres, superabsorbent polymers (sodium polyacrylate), additives, and trace residues including heavy metals, VOCs, phthalates, PFAS, and dioxins from bleaching. Internal products amplify chemical risk because vaginal tissue absorbs chemicals more readily than external skin.
- **Physics:** Conducted hands-on lab experiments measuring absorption capacity (g/g) and absorption rate (s per 5 mL) using a simulated fluid of water and glycerol. Three trials were run per product across seven products from three brands.
- **Environmental Science:** Analyzed global waste data (an estimated 80 billion products per menstrual cycle worldwide), decomposition timelines (approximately 800 years in landfills), and marine pollution impact (18,000 products wash up on beaches annually, contributing to roughly 100,000 marine animal deaths per year). Evaluated sustainable alternatives including reusable pads, menstrual cups, and period underwear.
- **Computer Science:** Built an interactive website presenting the research, including a recommendation tool with adjustable weighting sliders, radar charts comparing products across five axes, a correlation explorer, and product detail pages.

### Key findings

No single product is perfect. The group found a negative correlation of about -0.6 between performance and environmental impact: products that absorb better tend to be worse for the planet. The reusable cloth pad scored highest overall due to strong environmental and cost scores. Organic cotton pads were the best single-use alternative. The group also proposed an ideal product combining an organic cotton top layer, a high-absorbency core with fast wicking, a biodegradable shell, and PFAS-free waterproofing.

### Project website

The group built an interactive website showcasing their full research, data visualizations, and recommendation tool:

[Menstrual Products Comparison](https://menstrual-products-comparison.vercel.app/)

